## 3.3. NMR Structural Analysis
The major PS fractions in MP-PSC were analyzed by 2D NMR spectroscopy. Figure 2 shows the HSQC spectrum of MP-PSC and the results of the structural analysis are summarized in Table 2. Additionally, the ${}^{1}\mathrm{H},{}^{13}\mathrm{C},$ and HMBC spectra of MP-PSC are presented in Figures S3A,B and S4, respectively. It was found that Glc was bound in an $\alpha$ -1,4-Dglucan and neutral branched (acetylated) $\beta$ -D-glucomannan. From the intensities for the anomeric protons, it was concluded that the second PS was contained in smaller amounts. Intense through-bond resonances between H1 $(5.36\mathrm{ppm})$ , C2 $(74.4\mathrm{ppm})$ , C3 $(76.0\:\mathrm{ppm})$ , C4 $(80.1\mathrm{ppm})$ , and C5 $(74.1\mathrm{ppm})$ of $\alpha$ -D-Glcp were observed in the HMBC spectrum (Figure S4), proving the presence of $\alpha$ -1,4-D-glucan (glycogen) [70,71]. The following fragments were also identified: 1,4- $\cdot\beta$ -D-Glcp, 1,4- $\cdot\beta$ -D-Manp, 1,4,6- $\cdot\beta$ -D-Manp, and $\beta$ -DManp [72,73]. Based on inter-glycosidic interactions in the HMBC spectrum, it was found that $\beta$ -1,4-D-Glcp and $\beta$ -1,4,6-D-Manp were linked together by 1,4-glycosidic bonds [72]. A cross-peak was observed between C1 of $\beta{\mathrm{-}}1,4{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{D-}\mathrm{Glc}p$ and H4 of $\beta$ -1,4,6-D-Manp at $\delta105.7/3.84$ ppm in the HMBC spectrum. This was confirmed by the through-space internuclear spin–spin couplings at $4.52/3.84,4.80/3.73(5),$ and 4.80/3.63 ppm for H1/H4 correlation between $\beta$ -1,4-D-Glcp and $\beta$ -1,4,6-D-Manp, $\mathrm{H1/H4}(3)$ and H1/H5 interactions of $\beta$ -1,4,6-D-Manp and $\beta$ -1,4-D-Glcp in the ROESY spectrum. No definite interactions were found in the 2D spectra to prove that $\beta$ -1,4,6-D-Manp was replaced at O-6 by Man or the presence of 1,4-linked $\beta$ -D-homomanan due to the low Man content. Both types of binding exist in glucomannans [72].
The acetyl group-specific couplings between H and C from the methyl group at $2.16-$ 2.21/23.3 ppm were registered in the HSQC spectrum (Figure 2). The CO signal from the acetate resonating at 176.2 ppm was elucidated from the HMBC spectrum. The acetyl ester content in the sample was low, which agreed with the data in Table 1. The acetylation of Glc and Gal in MP-PSC was rejected, as it would lead to downfield chemical shifts, and it would be recorded in the HMBC and ROESY spectra. It was hypothesized that 1M2aonf i2n7 glucomannan was O-2 and/or O-3 acetylated, as demonstrated by Makarova et al. [73].
A high amount of Gal $(19.7\%)$ and small amounts of 3-O-Me-Gal $(2.7\%)$ and Fuc $(3.4\%)$ were detected in MP-PSC. The existence of a terminal $\alpha$ -L-Fucp was proved by the C1/H1, ${\cal C}5/\mathrm{H}5,$ and ${C6}/{\mathrm{H}6}$ cross-peaks in the HSQC spectrum at $104.1/5.07\$ , $72.0/4.18$ and $18.3/1.24\mathrm{ppm}$ (Figure 2), as well as by the intra-residue interactions between H1/H2 $(5.07/3.84\mathrm{ppm})$ and $\mathrm{H}5/\mathrm{H}6\left(4.18/1.24\mathrm{ppm}\right)$ within the ring in the COSY spectrum. In the HSQC spectrum, an interaction between the signals at 59.0 and $3.44\mathrm{ppm}$ was detected, which showed the presence of ${\mathrm{-OCH}}_{3}$ groups [74]. The substitution of O-3 with the methyl group at 1,6- $\cdot\alpha$ -D-Galp was demonstrated by intra-residue resonances between - ${\mathrm{-OCH}}_{3}$ at $3.44~\mathrm{ppm}$ and H3 $(3.53\mathrm{ppm})$ , H2 $(3.89\mathrm{ppm})$ , or H4 $(4.27\mathrm{ppm})$ of Gal in the ROESY spectrum. Couplings were found between $-\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$ and H3 of $\alpha$ -1,6-(3-O-Me)-D-Galp at $59.0/3.53\mathrm{ppm}$ or between C3 and ${\mathrm{-OCH}}_{3}$ at 81.7/3.44 ppm in the HMBC spectrum. C3 $(81.7\mathrm{ppm})$ of O-3 methylated $\alpha$ -1,6-D-Galp showed intense interactions with H1 $(4.98\mathrm{ppm})$ , $\mathrm{H}2\left(3.89\mathrm{ppm}\right)$ , and H4 $(4.27\mathrm{ppm})$ in the HMBC spectrum (Figure S4). The NMR analysis revealed that Gal and Fuc formed a heteropolymer in which $\alpha{\mathrm{-D-Gal}}p$ and its methylated derivative were linked by 1,6-glycosidic bonds, and they were partially substituted at O-2 by $\alpha$ -L-Fucp units, as reported by Komura et al. [74]. The existence of 1,6- $\alpha$ -D-Galp was proved by the intense through-bond resonances between H1 (C1) and C6 (H6, 6′) or C5 (H5) at 4.98 (100.7)/69.4 (3.70, 3.89) or 4.98 (100.7)/71.7 (4.18) ppm in the HMBC spectrum [75]. This was confirmed by the H1/H6, $6^{\prime}$ ; $\left(\mathrm{H1/H4}\right)$ , and H1/H5 throughspace internuclear spin–spin couplings at 4.98/3.70, 3.89; (4.98/4.02) and $4.98/4.18\mathrm{ppm}$ in the ROESY spectrum. The existence of the fragment $^{\rightarrow6}$ )- $\cdot\alpha$ -D-Ga $|p\rrangle$ -2-O- $\cdot\alpha$ -L-Fucp-( $^{1\rightarrow}$ in MP-PSC was revealed by the following inter-residue cross-peaks in the HMBC spectrum: H1 of $\alpha$ -L-Fucp with C2 of $\alpha{\mathrm{-}}1,2,6{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{D}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Gal}p$ at $5.07/80.8\mathrm{ppm},$ H1 of $\alpha$ -1,6-D-Galp with C6 of $\alpha{\mathrm{-}}1,2,6{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{D}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Gal}p$ at 4.98/69.4 ppm [75]. The couplings between H1 of $\alpha$ -L-Fucp and H2 of $\alpha$ -1,2,6-D-Galp at $5.07/3.84\mathrm{ppm}$ and the low-intense cross-peak for H1/H6 of $\alpha$ -1,2,6- $\scriptstyle\mathbf{D-Galp}$ at $5.13/3.70\ \mathrm{ppm}$ in the ROESY spectrum supported the O-2 substitution with Fuc of 1,6-linked $\scriptstyle\mathbf{D-Galp}$ . The ratio between the integral intensities of H1 of $\alpha$ -1,6-D-Galp $(4.98\mathrm{ppm})$ and H1 of $\alpha$ -1,2,6-D-Galp $(5.13\mathrm{ppm})$ was 81:19, which revealed that about $19\%$ of Gal was O-2 substituted. According to monosaccharide composition data, $19\%$ of the Gal content in MP-PSC was calculated as $3.7\%$ , which agreed with the Fuc content in the sample $(3.4\%)$ . Considering that the ratio between the integral intensities of H1 of $\alpha$ -1,2,6-D-Galp $(5.13\mathrm{ppm})$ and H1 of $\alpha$ -L-Fucp $(5.07\mathrm{ppm})$ was 62:38, it indicated that not only Fuc was attached to O-2 of Gal. An additional analysis after further chromatographic fractionation of MP-PSC will unambiguously prove or reject the existence of a covalent binding between, e.g., fucogalactan and glucomannan fragments elucidated in the MP-PSC.
Peaks resonating in the range $0.8\mathrm{-}3.0\mathrm{ppm},$ which are characteristic for the protons of the N-H and ${\mathrm{N-CH}}_{3}$ groups of proteins, were found in the proton spectra of MP-PSC. This was in agreement with the protein content in the complex (Table 1). Protein structures were not further investigated because this was beyond the scope of the current study, which was focused on PSs.
